## Lore

The Baalizad are newcomers to the surface of the Baalgor Wastelands. For as long as their elders can account for - they say ten thousand years - the whole people lived below ground in vast ant-like warrens, always moving on, always running from something they call the Demons of the Deep. A few centuries ago, well after the Elf-Dwarf War was over, the first of them dug up into daylight, and the people have since had to accept that there is nowhere further to run. If they are to last, they need friends above ground.

That has gone badly. Surface folk see a huge dark-shelled thing with a crab''s pincer where a hand should be, and the usual reaction is terror followed by violence. Coastal colonists, pirates and slavers attack on sight. Raiders also kill them hoping to wear the shell as armor, which is a mistake: it breaks up as it comes off the body. Some are hunted as monsters for sport and some are taken as slave labor. Unsurprisingly, the Baalizad have grown wary and bitter toward surface people in general.

They live in warrens, extended families of 20 to 50 with the largest reaching 250 or 300. A travelling warren leaves no open passage in its wake, for fear of pursuit. No living Baalizad has ever met a Demon of the Deep, and the young, comfortable on the surface, tend to treat the whole business as a creation myth while the elders keep warning that the old enemy is close and keep courting allies.

There is no division of labor among them: everyone digs and everyone fights, although those with two pincers drift toward soldiering and those with two clawed hands toward overseeing construction. Now and then a single Baalizad walks away from its warren to travel with outsiders, and cannot usually say why.

**Alignment:** Any, but they lean good or selfish.

**O.C.C.s available:** None as such. A player character is the equivalent of a Vagabond/Peasant Farmer and can never be anything else.

**Physical Appearance:** Big, slow and plated all over in dark shell, like an armored warrior from somewhere else entirely. The arms rarely match: a four-fingered claw hand on one side and a far larger pincer limb on the other.

**Size:** 7 feet (2.1 m).

**Weight:** 400-500 pounds (180-230 kg).

**Average Life Span:** About 100 years. One in a thousand lives past 800; these are thought of as near-immortal and hold great respect in the warren.

**Habitat:** Only the Baalgor Wastelands. Reports of them in the Yin-Sloth Jungles are sightings of the Fyr-Kree insectoids of the Great Fire Bog.

**Enemies:** None in particular beyond the ancestral, never-seen Demons of the Deep.

**Allies:** None in particular. Some humans, Eandroth, Gosai and Quorians have made friendly contact with Baalizad who roam the surface. They are open to anyone who is not savage and aggressive, which rules out most monster races and leaves humans, gnomes, elves, dwarves, troglodytes and the like.

## GM Notes

The entry makes the Baalizad a player character only with the G.M.''s approval, and then only as the equivalent of a Vagabond/Peasant Farmer. The experience page near the back of the book gives the Baalizad R.C.C. a column of its own.

What the race brings to that plain occupation is considerable: P.S. and P.E. on 5D6, 60 S.D.C., a natural A.R. of 14, a pincer that holds for 3D6 a round, and a breath weapon three times a day. Against that, P.P. is 2D6, walking speed is 2D4, the pincer hand cannot use tools, and outside the Wastelands the character is taken for a demon or a monster wherever it goes. Its speech is a problem at first as well, though it will have a working command of a new language within days.

The diet is a useful lever. A Baalizad eats rock and prizes gemstones as food, will not touch worked metal, and needs almost no water, which matters in a desert. Earth Warlocks are said to win Baalizad goodwill by turning dirt and clay into stone for them to eat.

Magic is close to meaningless to most of them, so they seldom want magic items, and a Baalizad can be no more than a minor psychic.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'baalizad');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'baalizad';

